// This Pine Script® code is subject to the terms of the Mozilla Public License 2.0 at https://mozilla.org/MPL/2.0/
// © midnightpickle
//
// ============================================================================
//  PVSRA × Camarilla Regime Strategy  🌙🥒   — BTC intraday, long + short
//  PHASE 1: harness + control arms. The real signal is added in Phase 2.
// ============================================================================
//
//  WHAT THIS IS
//    A research instrument, not a signal source. It exists to find out whether
//    the PVSRA vector-candle filter and the Camarilla levels contain anything,
//    built so that a NEGATIVE result is unambiguous. Every design choice below
//    trades apparent performance for falsifiability.
//
//  WHY THE CONTROL ARM SHIPS FIRST
//    There is no published backtest, study, or concrete rule set for PVSRA.
//    Camarilla's origin is folklore; the 1.1 and 1.168 constants have no
//    published derivation; the "80% reversal at H3/L3" figure is unverified
//    course marketing. So before measuring the signal, measure what RANDOM
//    entries do through this exact exit model, sizing, and cost assumption.
//    If the signal cannot beat a coin flip firing at the same rate, nothing
//    downstream matters. That comparison is only clean if both arms share one
//    file, which is why the control lives here rather than in a scratch copy.
//
//  PRE-REGISTERED PRIMARY HYPOTHESIS (fixed before any run)
//    Fade only, long + short, BINANCE:BTCUSDT.P, 5m,
//    climaxMult 2.0 / kATR 1.5 / tpR 1.5.
//    Everything else is exploratory and carries a multiple-comparisons
//    penalty against the honest trial count, INCLUDING abandoned runs.
//
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  FOUR DEFECTS CARRIED OVER FROM THE SOURCE INDICATORS (verified in the code)
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  1. ema_pvsr_master.pine:163 — `ta.sma(volume, 10)` includes the current bar.
//     Canonical PVSRA averages the PREVIOUS 10. As written a large bar inflates
//     its own threshold and suppresses its own signal.
//     HERE: `ta.sma(volume, n)[1]`, behind `useShiftedAvg` so the port can be
//     proven faithful in bug-compatible mode first. Bug fix, not a parameter.
//
//  2. ema_pvsr_master.pine:165 — `volumeSpread >= ta.highest(volumeSpread, 10)`
//     where the window contains the current bar, so it fires whenever the bar
//     sets a new 10-bar effort high: ~1 bar in 10 regardless of conditions, and
//     it dominates the 2x-volume leg.
//     HERE: `useSpreadLeg` defaults OFF. Measured as one A/B, never swept.
//
//  3. pivots_and_key_levels_master_.pine:575,581,587 — developing HTF extremes
//     via `request.security(..., lookahead=barmerge.lookahead_off)` with no
//     offset. On historical bars that returns the PREVIOUS completed period's
//     value; on realtime bars the LIVE developing value. Same line, two
//     different quantities, so backtest and live are different strategies.
//     Harmless in an indicator. Fatal in a strategy.
//     HERE: HARD RULE — this file contains NO `request.security` at all. Every
//     level is computed natively on the chart timeframe. Do not add one.
//     (The source's prior-period calls at :571 and :577 use `[1]` plus
//     lookahead_on, which IS the documented non-repainting idiom and is fine.
//     The blanket ban is so defect 3 cannot regress via "just one more level".)
//
//  4. pivots_and_key_levels_master_.pine:235 — stores r1=H2, r2=H3, r3=H4 and
//     drops H1. Every published source maps R1->H1 one-to-one, so that script's
//     R2 is the classic FADE level and its R3 is the classic BREAK level.
//     Reading R3 as "far extreme, rarely reached" gets it exactly backwards.
//     HERE: semantic names only — camFadeHi/Lo, camBreakHi/Lo, camAnchor.
//     Never r*/s*.
//
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  COST ARITHMETIC — the numbers any result must be checked against
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  Bybit perp taker 0.055%/side, Binance 0.05% (0.045% w/ BNB), maker 0.02%.
//  `commission_value` is charged PER SIDE.
//  Breakeven win rate  W = (L + f) / (T + L)
//  with L = 0.69% (0.275 x a 2.5% prior daily range) and f = 0.11% fees
//  + 0.06% slippage = 0.17%:
//
//     tpR    target    pre-cost BE    post-cost BE    drag
//     1.0    0.69%        50.0%          62.3%       12.3 pp
//     1.5    1.04%        40.0%          49.9%        9.9 pp
//     2.0    1.38%        33.3%          41.5%        8.2 pp
//
//  Drag shrinks as tpR rises, which is the argument against small targets.
//  A backtest reporting 55% at tpR 1.5 claims only ~5 pp over breakeven —
//  well inside what slippage misspecification alone can manufacture.
//
//  SLIPPAGE IS THE SETTING MOST LIKELY TO FAKE AN EDGE.
//  It is a const int IN TICKS. BTC mintick is 0.1, so at $110,000:
//      3 bps = $33 = 330 ticks.
//      The tutorial default `slippage = 2` = $0.20 = 0.018 bps ≈ free.
//  That is a ~165x error and it silently turns losers into winners. Pine
//  cannot make the declaration adaptive, so this file computes the suggested
//  tick count at runtime and shouts in the diagnostics table when the declared
//  constant has drifted from it. Signals fire on volatility expansion by
//  construction, so realised slippage here is WORSE than the symbol average:
//  3 bps is a starting point, not a conservative one.
//
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  STOP MODEL — note the correction
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  The claim that the Camarilla stop is "far tighter than convention" is
//  measured against DAILY ATR. Against 5m ATR(14) (~0.10-0.15%) the structural
//  distance (~0.69%) is 5-8x — comfortably WIDER than the 1.5-2.0x convention.
//  So `max(structural, kATR*ATR)` is a FLOOR against degenerate small-range
//  days, not a widener. The opposite exposure (wide stops on big-range days)
//  is handled by fixed-fractional sizing, not by a cap: a cap would break the
//  structural logic and cost another free parameter.
//
// ============================================================================

//@version=6
strategy(
     title                        = "PVSRA × Camarilla Regime Strategy 🌙🥒",
     shorttitle                   = "PVSRA-CAM",
     overlay                      = true,
     pyramiding                   = 0,
     initial_capital              = 100000,
     currency                     = currency.USD,
     default_qty_type             = strategy.fixed,
     default_qty_value            = 1,
     commission_type              = strategy.commission.percent,
     commission_value             = 0.055,
     slippage                     = 330,
     margin_long                  = 100,
     margin_short                 = 100,
     process_orders_on_close      = false,
     calc_on_every_tick           = false,
     calc_on_order_fills          = false,
     fill_orders_on_standard_ohlc = true,
     max_bars_back                = 1000,
     max_labels_count             = 500)

// ============================================================================
// 🔹 CONSTANTS — deliberately NOT inputs
// ============================================================================
// Exposing the Camarilla coefficients turns a structural hypothesis into a
// curve-fit. They are the most overfittable knobs in the whole design and they
// have no published derivation. To find out whether they matter, use the
// random-level control arm, not a sweep.
const float CAM_K         = 1.1
const float CAM_EXT       = 1.168
const int   DECLARED_SLIP = 330      // MUST equal strategy(slippage=) above

// ============================================================================
// 🔹 INPUTS
// ============================================================================

// --- 00 · Backtest window -----------------------------------------------
g00 = "00 · Backtest Window"
windowMode = input.string("In-Sample", "Window", options=["In-Sample", "Out-of-Sample", "All"], group=g00,
     tooltip="Select parameters on In-Sample ONLY. Out-of-Sample is one shot, after the design is frozen.")
isStart  = input.time(timestamp("01 Jan 2022 00:00 +0000"), "IS start",  group=g00)
isEnd    = input.time(timestamp("31 Dec 2023 23:59 +0000"), "IS end",    group=g00)
oosStart = input.time(timestamp("01 Jan 2024 00:00 +0000"), "OOS start", group=g00)
oosEnd   = input.time(timestamp("31 Dec 2025 23:59 +0000"), "OOS end",   group=g00)

// --- 01 · Mode -----------------------------------------------------------
g01 = "01 · Mode"
tradeMode = input.string("Fade only", "Trade mode", options=["Fade only", "Break only", "Both"], group=g01,
     tooltip="Pre-registered primary hypothesis is 'Fade only'. Changing this is a new trial — write it down.")
dirMode   = input.string("Long + Short", "Direction", options=["Long + Short", "Long only", "Short only"], group=g01)

// --- 02 · Levels ---------------------------------------------------------
g02 = "02 · Levels"
anchorHourUTC = input.int(0, "Day boundary (UTC hour)", minval=0, maxval=23, group=g02,
     tooltip="FIXED at 0. Every level moves when this moves, and TradingView feeds disagree about the crypto day boundary. Swept only in the venue-robustness check.")
showLevels    = input.bool(true, "Plot levels", group=g02)

// --- 03 · PVSRA ----------------------------------------------------------
// Phase 2 consumes these. Declared now so the input panel is stable between
// phases and saved chart settings survive the upgrade.
g03 = "03 · PVSRA"
climaxMult    = input.float(2.0, "Climax volume ×",    minval=1.0, maxval=5.0, step=0.1, group=g03) // FREE #1
aboveavgMult  = input.float(1.5, "Above-average ×",    minval=1.0, maxval=3.0, step=0.1, group=g03) // fixed (inert at minGrade 3)
volLookback   = input.int(10,    "Volume lookback",    minval=2,   maxval=100, step=1,   group=g03) // fixed (canonical)
minGrade      = input.int(3,     "Minimum grade",      minval=2,   maxval=3,             group=g03) // fixed
useShiftedAvg = input.bool(true, "Shift average off current bar", group=g03,
     tooltip="ON = corrected (canonical). OFF = bug-compatible with the source indicator, for the port-fidelity test only.")
useSpreadLeg  = input.bool(false, "Use volume×spread 10-bar-high leg", group=g03,
     tooltip="OFF by default. This leg fires ~1 bar in 10 regardless of conditions and dominates the volume leg. One A/B, never swept.")

// --- 04 · Risk -----------------------------------------------------------
g04 = "04 · Risk"
stopMode     = input.string("Max", "Stop model", options=["Max", "Structural", "ATR"], group=g04,
     tooltip="Max = floor the structural distance with kATR×ATR, guarding degenerate small-range days.")
kATR         = input.float(1.5, "ATR multiple", minval=0.5, maxval=5.0, step=0.25, group=g04) // FREE #2
atrLen       = input.int(14, "ATR length", minval=5, maxval=50, group=g04)                    // fixed
riskPct      = input.float(1.0, "Risk per trade %", minval=0.1, maxval=5.0, step=0.1, group=g04)
riskBaseMode = input.string("Flat", "Risk base", options=["Flat", "Compounding"], group=g04,
     tooltip="Flat removes compounding. Compounding inflates Sharpe and hides that most P&L came from late, large trades. It is a deployment decision, not an evaluation one.")

// --- 05 · Exits ----------------------------------------------------------
g05 = "05 · Exits"
tpMode           = input.string("R", "Target model", options=["R", "Structural"], group=g05,
     tooltip="R keeps the breakeven arithmetic closed-form. Structural is more authentic to the method but makes every aggregate statistic a mixture over varying R.")
tpR              = input.float(1.5, "Target (R)", minval=0.5, maxval=5.0, step=0.25, group=g05) // FREE #3
maxBarsInTrade   = input.int(24, "Time stop (bars)", minval=3, maxval=200, group=g05,
     tooltip="The only thing stopping a fade trade riding into a trend all day.")
flattenOnDayRoll = input.bool(true, "Flatten on day roll", group=g05,
     tooltip="At the roll every level the trade was predicated on ceases to exist. Also bounds the unmodelled perp funding exposure.")
cooldownBars     = input.int(12, "Cooldown after exit (bars)", minval=0, maxval=100, group=g05)

// --- 06 · Costs & diagnostics -------------------------------------------
g06 = "06 · Costs & Diagnostics"
slipBps   = input.float(3.0, "Assumed slippage (bps/side)", minval=0.0, maxval=50.0, step=0.5, group=g06)
showTable = input.bool(true, "Show diagnostics table", group=g06)

// --- 07 · Control arms ---------------------------------------------------
g07 = "07 · Control Arms"
controlMode = input.string("Random entry", "Control arm",
     options=["Off", "Random entry", "Random level", "Random PVSRA"], group=g07,
     tooltip="Phase 1 ships with 'Random entry' active. This establishes the null distribution the real signal must beat.")
controlSeed         = input.int(1, "Seed", minval=1, maxval=999, group=g07,
     tooltip="Run 20+ seeds. A single seed is an anecdote.")
controlTradesPerDay = input.float(1.0, "Target trades/day", minval=0.05, maxval=20.0, step=0.05, group=g07,
     tooltip="Phase 2 auto-calibrates this to the live arm's realised rate. Until then, set it to match.")

// ============================================================================
// 🔹 UTILITIES
// ============================================================================

// Deterministic hash PRNG in [0,1). Same bar always yields the same value, so
// the control arm is reproducible and the trade list diffs clean across runs.
f_rand(int seed, int n, int salt) =>
    float v = math.sin(seed * 12.9898 + n * 78.2331 + salt * 37.7191) * 43758.5453
    v - math.floor(v)

f_bps2ticks(float bps) =>
    math.round(close * bps / 10000.0 / syminfo.mintick)

// ============================================================================
// 🔹 BACKTEST WINDOW
// ============================================================================
inWindow = windowMode == "All"      ? true
         : windowMode == "In-Sample" ? (time >= isStart  and time <= isEnd)
         :                             (time >= oosStart and time <= oosEnd)

// ============================================================================
// 🔹 DAY ANCHOR + PRIOR-DAY OHLC  (native — no request.security)
// ============================================================================
// Derived from the UTC hour rather than ta.change(time('D')) so the boundary is
// EXPLICIT and feed-independent. Every level in this strategy is a function of
// it, and TradingView feeds disagree about where the crypto day starts.

int  utcH   = hour(time, "UTC")
bool newDay = utcH == anchorHourUTC and utcH[1] != anchorHourUTC

var float curH = na
var float curL = na
var float pdH  = na
var float pdL  = na
var float pdC  = na

if newDay
    // SNAPSHOT FIRST, THEN RESET. Reversing these two blocks silently yields a
    // one-bar prior range and levels that still look entirely plausible.
    pdH  := curH
    pdL  := curL
    pdC  := close[1]
    curH := high
    curL := low
else
    curH := math.max(nz(curH, high), high)
    curL := math.min(nz(curL, low),  low)

float pdRange = nz(pdH, na) - nz(pdL, na)
bool  hasLvls = not na(pdC) and not na(pdH) and not na(pdL) and pdRange > 0

// ============================================================================
// 🔹 CAMARILLA — semantic names only (see defect 4)
// ============================================================================
// Ordering invariant whenever pdRange > 0:
//   camBreakLo < camFadeLo < camAnchor < camFadeHi < camBreakHi

float camAnchor  = pdC
float camPivot   = hasLvls ? (pdH + pdL + pdC) / 3.0 : na   // reported only
float camFadeHi  = hasLvls ? pdC + pdRange * CAM_K / 4.0 : na
float camFadeLo  = hasLvls ? pdC - pdRange * CAM_K / 4.0 : na
float camBreakHi = hasLvls ? pdC + pdRange * CAM_K / 2.0 : na
float camBreakLo = hasLvls ? pdC - pdRange * CAM_K / 2.0 : na
float camTgtHi   = hasLvls ? camBreakHi + CAM_EXT * (camBreakHi - camFadeHi) : na  // plot only
float camTgtLo   = hasLvls ? camBreakLo - CAM_EXT * (camFadeLo - camBreakLo) : na  // plot only

// ============================================================================
// 🔹 CONTEXT SERIES
// ============================================================================
float atr = ta.atr(atrLen)

// ============================================================================
// 🔹 REGIME PARTITION
// ============================================================================
// beyondUp / beyondDn / inside PARTITION the real line, so exactly one is true
// on every bar. That is the proof of mutual exclusivity: fade requires `inside`
// and break requires one of the other two, so they cannot co-fire; and
// break-long and break-short cannot co-fire for the same reason.

bool beyondUp = hasLvls and close > camBreakHi
bool beyondDn = hasLvls and close < camBreakLo
bool inside   = hasLvls and not beyondUp and not beyondDn

// ============================================================================
// 🔹 SIGNAL  —  PHASE 2 POPULATES THIS
// ============================================================================
// Phase 1 deliberately produces no live signal. Everything downstream reads
// only sigDir and sigMode, so the control arms below exercise the exact exit
// model, sizing and cost path that the real signal will later use. That shared
// path is the entire point of building the control first.

int sigDir  = 0    // -1 short, 0 none, +1 long
int sigMode = 0    //  0 none, 1 fade, 2 break

// ============================================================================
// 🔹 CONTROL ARMS
// ============================================================================
// Overwrites sigDir/sigMode when active. Fires at `controlTradesPerDay` through
// the identical exit model, sizing, costs and window as the live arm.

int   barsPerDay  = math.max(1, int(86400 / math.max(1, timeframe.in_seconds())))
float perBarProb  = controlTradesPerDay / barsPerDay

if controlMode == "Random entry" and hasLvls
    float r1 = f_rand(controlSeed, bar_index, 1)
    if r1 < perBarProb
        float r2 = f_rand(controlSeed, bar_index, 2)
        sigDir  := r2 < 0.5 ? 1 : -1
        sigMode := 1

// Direction gate applies to every arm equally.
if (dirMode == "Long only" and sigDir == -1) or (dirMode == "Short only" and sigDir == 1)
    sigDir  := 0
    sigMode := 0

// ============================================================================
// 🔹 SNAPSHOT + SIZING
// ============================================================================
// Distances are frozen at SIGNAL time. Absolute stop/target prices are set from
// the ACTUAL fill once the position opens, because the order fills at the next
// bar's open, not at the signal close.
//
// The exit block must never recompute from live camFade*/camBreak*. A trade
// surviving the day roll would have its stop and target teleported onto the new
// day's levels, exiting at prices with no relationship to the entry — and the
// equity curve would still look entirely reasonable.

var int   pendDir      = 0
var int   pendMode     = 0
var float pendStopDist = na
var float pendTpDist   = na
var float pendQty      = na

var float stopPrice = na
var float tpPrice   = na
var int   entryBar  = na
var int   entryHour = na
var int   lastExitBar = -99999

bool cooledDown = na(lastExitBar) or (bar_index - lastExitBar) >= cooldownBars
bool flat       = strategy.position_size == 0

if sigDir != 0 and hasLvls and inWindow and flat and cooledDown
    // Structural distance: fade measures to the break level it is fading
    // against; break measures back to the fade level it just cleared.
    float structDist = sigMode == 1
         ? (sigDir > 0 ? math.abs(close - camBreakLo) : math.abs(camBreakHi - close))
         : (sigDir > 0 ? math.abs(close - camFadeHi)  : math.abs(camFadeLo - close))
    float atrDist = kATR * atr
    float sd = stopMode == "Structural" ? structDist
             : stopMode == "ATR"        ? atrDist
             :                            math.max(structDist, atrDist)

    if not na(sd) and sd > 0
        float structTgt = sigMode == 1
             ? math.abs(camAnchor - close)
             : math.abs((sigDir > 0 ? camBreakHi - camFadeHi : camFadeLo - camBreakLo))
        float td = tpMode == "R" ? tpR * sd : math.max(structTgt, syminfo.mintick)

        float riskBase = riskBaseMode == "Flat" ? strategy.initial_capital : strategy.equity
        float qty      = (riskBase * riskPct / 100.0) / sd

        if qty > 0
            pendDir      := sigDir
            pendMode     := sigMode
            pendStopDist := sd
            pendTpDist   := td
            pendQty      := qty
            if sigDir > 0
                strategy.entry("L", strategy.long,  qty=qty, comment="L" + (sigMode == 1 ? "-fade" : "-break"))
            else
                strategy.entry("S", strategy.short, qty=qty, comment="S" + (sigMode == 1 ? "-fade" : "-break"))

// ============================================================================
// 🔹 ORDERS — one exit call carrying BOTH stop and limit
// ============================================================================
// Two separate exit orders are two independent orders in the emulator and can
// produce double-closes and nonsensical sequencing. A single order lets the
// emulator apply its own same-bar resolution — which is then MEASURED below
// via bothHitFrac rather than trusted.

bool justOpened = strategy.position_size != 0 and strategy.position_size[1] == 0
bool justClosed = strategy.position_size == 0 and strategy.position_size[1] != 0

if justOpened
    float fill = strategy.position_avg_price
    bool  isLong = strategy.position_size > 0
    stopPrice := isLong ? fill - pendStopDist : fill + pendStopDist
    tpPrice   := isLong ? fill + pendTpDist   : fill - pendTpDist
    entryBar  := bar_index
    entryHour := utcH

if strategy.position_size != 0 and not na(stopPrice) and not na(tpPrice)
    strategy.exit("X", from_entry = strategy.position_size > 0 ? "L" : "S",
         stop = stopPrice, limit = tpPrice,
         comment_profit = "TP", comment_loss = "SL")

// Time stop — the only thing preventing a fade riding into a trend all day.
if strategy.position_size != 0 and not na(entryBar) and (bar_index - entryBar) >= maxBarsInTrade
    strategy.close_all(comment = "TIME")

// Day-roll flatten — past the roll the thesis no longer exists.
if flattenOnDayRoll and newDay and strategy.position_size != 0
    strategy.close_all(comment = "ROLL")

// ============================================================================
// 🔹 DIAGNOSTICS
// ============================================================================
// bothHitFrac is the single most important honesty metric here: it quantifies
// how much of the result rests on the emulator's same-bar assumption rather
// than on price action.
//   FALSIFICATION THRESHOLD: bothHitFrac > 0.15 means the backtest is fiction
//   and the result must be re-derived on finer data.

var int nTrades  = 0
var int nBothHit = 0
var int nLong    = 0
var int nShort   = 0

if justClosed
    nTrades := nTrades + 1
    if not na(stopPrice) and not na(tpPrice)
        float lo = math.min(stopPrice, tpPrice)
        float hi = math.max(stopPrice, tpPrice)
        if low <= lo and high >= hi
            nBothHit := nBothHit + 1
    if strategy.position_size[1] > 0
        nLong := nLong + 1
    else
        nShort := nShort + 1
    lastExitBar := bar_index
    stopPrice   := na
    tpPrice     := na
    entryBar    := na

int   suggestedTicks = f_bps2ticks(slipBps)
bool  slipMismatch   = math.abs(DECLARED_SLIP - suggestedTicks) > math.max(1, suggestedTicks) * 0.25
float bothHitFrac    = nTrades > 0 ? nBothHit / float(nTrades) : 0.0

if showTable and barstate.islast
    var table t = table.new(position.top_right, 2, 12, border_width = 1)
    f_row(int r, string k, string v, color c) =>
        table.cell(t, 0, r, k, text_size = size.small, text_halign = text.align_left,  bgcolor = color.new(color.black, 70), text_color = color.silver)
        table.cell(t, 1, r, v, text_size = size.small, text_halign = text.align_right, bgcolor = color.new(color.black, 70), text_color = c)

    f_row(0,  "ARM",            controlMode == "Off" ? "LIVE SIGNAL" : controlMode, controlMode == "Off" ? color.lime : color.orange)
    f_row(1,  "seed",           str.tostring(controlSeed), color.silver)
    f_row(2,  "window",         windowMode, color.silver)
    f_row(3,  "trades",         str.tostring(nTrades), nTrades >= 150 ? color.lime : color.orange)
    f_row(4,  "long / short",   str.tostring(nLong) + " / " + str.tostring(nShort), color.silver)
    f_row(5,  "bothHitFrac",    str.tostring(bothHitFrac, "#.###"), bothHitFrac > 0.15 ? color.red : color.lime)
    f_row(6,  "declared slip",  str.tostring(DECLARED_SLIP) + " ticks", slipMismatch ? color.red : color.lime)
    f_row(7,  "suggested slip", str.tostring(suggestedTicks) + " ticks (" + str.tostring(slipBps, "#.#") + " bps)", slipMismatch ? color.red : color.silver)
    f_row(8,  "mintick",        str.tostring(syminfo.mintick), color.silver)
    f_row(9,  "bars/day",       str.tostring(barsPerDay), color.silver)
    f_row(10, "net profit",     str.tostring(strategy.netprofit, "#.##"), strategy.netprofit > 0 ? color.lime : color.red)
    f_row(11, "PF",             str.tostring(strategy.grossprofit / math.max(strategy.grossloss, 1), "#.###"), color.silver)

// ============================================================================
// 🔹 PLOTS
// ============================================================================
plot(showLevels and hasLvls ? camBreakHi : na, "camBreakHi", color.new(color.red,    20), 2, plot.style_linebr)
plot(showLevels and hasLvls ? camFadeHi  : na, "camFadeHi",  color.new(color.orange, 20), 1, plot.style_linebr)
plot(showLevels and hasLvls ? camAnchor  : na, "camAnchor",  color.new(color.gray,   40), 1, plot.style_linebr)
plot(showLevels and hasLvls ? camFadeLo  : na, "camFadeLo",  color.new(color.orange, 20), 1, plot.style_linebr)
plot(showLevels and hasLvls ? camBreakLo : na, "camBreakLo", color.new(color.green,  20), 2, plot.style_linebr)

bgcolor(beyondUp ? color.new(color.green, 92) : beyondDn ? color.new(color.red, 92) : na, title="Regime")
